{"content_hash": "64533ead33614af1", "version_id": 513, "article_id": 52, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "UK court clears ex-Deutsche Bank trader in rate case", "title_zh": "英国法院推翻德意志银行前交易员的利率操纵定罪", "has_translation": true, "topic": "business", "source": "bbc_business", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqgkvj10k6lno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1620266757065-5814239881fd?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8Mnx8ZGV1dHNjaGUlMjBiYW5rJTIwdHJhZGVyfGVufDB8MHx8fDE3OTE2Nzc5NDl8MA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Kanchanara on Unsplash", "author": "Kanchanara", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@kanchanara?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/person-using-black-laptop-computer-5hcV51EeeWc?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(271, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(309, 46%, 48%)", "label": "BUSINESS", "seed": "14bf80248d84"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 182, "reading_minutes": 0.9, "lead": "A former Deutsche Bank trader has won a long legal fight to clear his name. The Court of Appeal has overturned his conviction for rate rigging, and many other bankers have also been cleared.", "lead_zh": "一名德意志银行前交易员经过漫长的法律斗争，终于洗清了自己的罪名。上诉法院推翻了他操纵利率的定罪，许多其他银行家也已被宣告无罪。", "preview": "Christian Bittar went to prison in 2018 for manipulating Euribor, a key interest rate. However, the Court of Appeal has now quashed his conv", "preview_zh": "克里斯蒂安·比塔尔因操纵欧元银行间同业拆借利率（Euribor）这一关键利率，于2018年入狱。然而，上诉法院现已推翻了他的定罪。由于未能获得入境英国的签证，他通过视频连线从瑞士观看了庭审。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 501, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/52/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 72.2, "size_bytes": 436896, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 207, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fc48a26/content/data/versions/513.json", "paragraphs": ["Christian Bittar went to prison in 2018 for manipulating Euribor, a key interest rate. However, the Court of Appeal has now quashed his conviction. He watched the hearing by video link from Switzerland because he could not get a visa to enter the UK.", "Earlier this week, the same court also cleared five former Barclays bankers. Since 2015, nineteen City traders were convicted in the US and UK, but eighteen of them have now been acquitted. Only one former trader, Peter Johnson, still has a conviction, although he has applied to appeal.", "Bittar said he had waited a very long time for this day. His wife Caroline said their family had lost fifteen valuable years, and their children grew up under this injustice. David Davis, a former minister, said the traders were victims of a scapegoating exercise.", "The scandal began after the 2008 financial crisis. Banks were accused of misreporting their borrowing rates to boost profits. In 2025, the Supreme Court ruled that judges had misdirected juries, and that whether the requests were wrong was a question of fact for the jury."], "paragraphs_zh": ["克里斯蒂安·比塔尔因操纵欧元银行间同业拆借利率（Euribor）这一关键利率，于2018年入狱。然而，上诉法院现已推翻了他的定罪。由于未能获得入境英国的签证，他通过视频连线从瑞士观看了庭审。", "本周早些时候，同一法院还宣告五名前巴克莱银行家无罪。自2015年以来，共有19名伦敦金融城交易员在美国和英国被定罪，但其中18人现已被宣告无罪。目前只剩一名前交易员彼得·约翰逊仍有定罪，不过他也已申请上诉。", "比塔尔说，他为这一天等了非常非常久。他的妻子卡罗琳说，他们一家失去了15年宝贵时光，孩子们在這種不公下长大。前大臣戴维·戴维斯表示，这些交易员是一场替罪羊行动的受害者。", "这场丑闻始于2008年金融危机之后。银行被指控虚报借款利率以抬高利润。2025年，最高法院裁定法官误导了陪审团，而请求是否错误应由陪审团根据事实来裁定。"], "body": "Christian Bittar went to prison in 2018 for manipulating Euribor, a key interest rate. However, the Court of Appeal has now quashed his conviction. He watched the hearing by video link from Switzerland because he could not get a visa to enter the UK.\n\nEarlier this week, the same court also cleared five former Barclays bankers. Since 2015, nineteen City traders were convicted in the US and UK, but eighteen of them have now been acquitted. Only one former trader, Peter Johnson, still has a conviction, although he has applied to appeal.\n\nBittar said he had waited a very long time for this day. His wife Caroline said their family had lost fifteen valuable years, and their children grew up under this injustice. David Davis, a former minister, said the traders were victims of a scapegoating exercise.\n\nThe scandal began after the 2008 financial crisis. Banks were accused of misreporting their borrowing rates to boost profits. In 2025, the Supreme Court ruled that judges had misdirected juries, and that whether the requests were wrong was a question of fact for the jury.", "body_zh": "克里斯蒂安·比塔尔因操纵欧元银行间同业拆借利率（Euribor）这一关键利率，于2018年入狱。然而，上诉法院现已推翻了他的定罪。由于未能获得入境英国的签证，他通过视频连线从瑞士观看了庭审。\n\n本周早些时候，同一法院还宣告五名前巴克莱银行家无罪。自2015年以来，共有19名伦敦金融城交易员在美国和英国被定罪，但其中18人现已被宣告无罪。目前只剩一名前交易员彼得·约翰逊仍有定罪，不过他也已申请上诉。\n\n比塔尔说，他为这一天等了非常非常久。他的妻子卡罗琳说，他们一家失去了15年宝贵时光，孩子们在這種不公下长大。前大臣戴维·戴维斯表示，这些交易员是一场替罪羊行动的受害者。\n\n这场丑闻始于2008年金融危机之后。银行被指控虚报借款利率以抬高利润。2025年，最高法院裁定法官误导了陪审团，而请求是否错误应由陪审团根据事实来裁定。", "text": "Christian Bittar went to prison in 2018 for manipulating Euribor, a key interest rate. However, the Court of Appeal has now quashed his conviction. He watched the hearing by video link from Switzerland because he could not get a visa to enter the UK. Earlier this week, the same court also cleared five former Barclays bankers. Since 2015, nineteen City traders were convicted in the US and UK, but eighteen of them have now been acquitted. Only one former trader, Peter Johnson, still has a conviction, although he has applied to appeal. Bittar said he had waited a very long time for this day. His wife Caroline said their family had lost fifteen valuable years, and their children grew up under this injustice. David Davis, a former minister, said the traders were victims of a scapegoating exercise. The scandal began after the 2008 financial crisis. Banks were accused of misreporting their borrowing rates to boost profits. In 2025, the Supreme Court ruled that judges had misdirected juries, and that whether the requests were wrong was a question of fact for the jury.", "vocab": [{"word": "overturn", "pos": "verb", "zh": "推翻", "note": "常用于法律语境，指推翻判决或决定", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "conviction", "pos": "noun", "zh": "定罪", "note": "法律用语，指判定有罪", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "quash", "pos": "verb", "zh": "撤销", "note": "正式用语，指法院撤销原判", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "acquit", "pos": "verb", "zh": "宣告无罪", "note": "常用被动语态：be acquitted of", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "appeal", "pos": "noun/verb", "zh": "上诉", "note": "法律用语，指请求上级法院复审", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "manipulate", "pos": "verb", "zh": "操纵", "note": "指不正当控制或影响", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "scandal", "pos": "noun", "zh": "丑闻", "note": "指引起公愤的不道德或违法行为", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "benchmark", "pos": "noun", "zh": "基准", "note": "常用于金融领域，如基准利率", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "jury", "pos": "noun", "zh": "陪审团", "note": "法律用语，由公民组成裁决事实", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "scapegoat", "pos": "noun/verb", "zh": "替罪羊", "note": "指替别人承担罪责的人", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Ex-Deutsche Bank trader jailed for rigging rates has conviction overturned", "summary_original": "The Court of Appeal quashes the conviction of Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader jailed in 2018.", "body_original": "Ex-Deutsche Bank trader jailed for rigging rates has conviction overturned\nBy\nAndy Verity\nFinancial investigations correspondent\nPublished\n9 October 2026\nAnother former banker sentenced in one of the biggest scandals of the financial crisis has had his conviction overturned following a long-running legal battle.\nThe Court of Appeal has quashed the conviction of Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader who went to prison in 2018 for \"manipulating\" the benchmark interest rate, Euribor.\nIt comes after the same UK court overturned five convictions of ex-Barclays bankers in the rate rigging trials earlier this week.\nBittar, who watched proceedings via video link from Switzerland because he was not granted a visa to attend court, told the BBC: \"I have waited a very, very long time for this day.\"\nThe financial crisis began in 2008, sending huge economic shockwaves across the world and triggering recessions in many countries.\nThere was a public backlash against bankers, held by many to be responsible for the crisis, while the financial sector was protected by taxpayer-funded bailouts.\nThe Libor scandal erupted in 2012, when it was discovered that at the outbreak of the financial crisis, banks had been misrepresenting their positions during the process of setting the lending rate, helping to boost profits and mask difficulties.\nSome 19 City traders were convicted in the US and UK between 2015 and 2019 across nine criminal trials held in London and New York.\nEighteen of these have now been acquitted.\nOnly one former trader in the world now remains convicted: former Barclays trader Peter Johnson, who was also one of the original whistleblowers in the interest rate rigging scandal. He pleaded guilty on advice that he had little chance at winning at trial. The court confirmed that he too has now applied to appeal against his conviction.\nThe news has already prompted lawyers and senior politicians to press for the Bank of England and the Treasury to release all their records about their own roles in interest rate 'rigging' during the financial crisis, amid concerns raised of a cover-up of the role of central banks and governments.\n'Finally our injustice has been recognised'\nSpeaking after his conviction was quashed, Bittar said: \"Finally the injustice of what I and others suffered has been recognised.\n\"I am so grateful for those who stood by me through this ordeal and those who worked so tirelessly to correct it.\"\nHis wife, Caroline, who lives in the UK, said their family had lost 15 \"valuable years\", with their children \"growing up with this injustice\".\nShe added they were looking forward to \"enjoying life with our family and friends without this shadow hanging over us\".\nOn Wednesday, Jay Merchant, Jonathan Mathew, Alex Pabon, Colin Bermingham and Philippe Moryoussef saw their convictions for 'manipulating' the interest rate benchmarks Libor and Euribor quashed after a 10-year struggle for justice.\nThey launched fresh appeals against their jail sentences following a Supreme Court ruling last year that acquitted Tom Hayes, the first to be jailed for 'rigging' interest rates in 2015, and Carlo Palombo, jailed in 2019.\nTom Hayes, who was initially sentenced to 14 years, reduced on appeal to 11, is now seeking damages from his former employer UBS.\nThe traders were among 37 City traders and brokers prosecuted for \"manipulating\" the interest rate benchmarks Libor and Euribor, which track the cost of borrowing cash between the banks and have been used to set the interest rates on millions of mortgages and commercial loans.\nThe BBC has uncovered evidence of a much larger, state-led 'rigging' of interest rates, under pressure from central banks and governments across the world. Evidence implicating Downing Street and the Bank of England was suppressed throughout the criminal trials.\nFormer Conservative cabinet minister David Davis told the BBC the innocent traders had been the victims of a series of miscarriages of justice.\nHe said the people exonerated this week and before were \"the victims of a scapegoating exercise which arose as a result of the government's own misbehaviour in lowballing [falsely understating] Libor interest rates themselves, in order to try and rescue the economy after their own self-induced crash in the late 2000s\".\nIn 2015, during the first trial for interest rate 'rigging', of former UBS trader Hayes, Mr Justice Jeremy Cooke decided as a matter of law that any attempt to influence the rates could not be lawful, and that any rate influenced by commercial interest must automatically be false. Judges in the subsequent eight trials followed suit.\nHowever, in July 2025, the Supreme Court decided that all the judges had erred, misdirecting juries that it was wrong as a matter of law. Instead, whether the requests were right or wrong was properly a matter of fact for the jury to decide, not the judge.\nAll convictions in the United States were overturned in 2022 after an appeal court ruled that there was insufficient evidence that the traders' requests had broken any rules or laws.\nRelated topics\nUK Supreme Court\nDeutsche Bank\nBarclays\nSerious Fraud Office\nBanking\nMore on this story\nEx-bankers jailed for rigging rates have convictions quashed\nPublished\n3 days ago\nRate 'rigging' traders say they were scapegoated - now the Supreme Court will decide\nPublished\n26 May 2025", "fetched_at": "2026-10-11T00:19:08.412879", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Christian", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.588, "sm": 100, "em": 587, "cs": 0, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Bittar", "s": 0.6, "e": 1.038, "sm": 600, "em": 1037, "cs": 10, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "went", "s": 1.05, "e": 1.225, "sm": 1050, "em": 1225, "cs": 17, "ce": 21, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "to", "s": 1.238, "e": 1.338, "sm": 1237, "em": 1337, "cs": 22, "ce": 24, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "prison", "s": 1.35, "e": 1.738, "sm": 1350, "em": 1737, "cs": 25, "ce": 31, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "in", "s": 1.75, "e": 2.112, "sm": 1750, "em": 2112, "cs": 32, "ce": 34, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "2018", "s": 2.112, "e": 2.837, "sm": 2112, "em": 2837, "cs": 35, "ce": 39, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "for", "s": 2.85, "e": 2.988, "sm": 2850, "em": 2987, "cs": 40, "ce": 43, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "manipulating", "s": 3.0, "e": 3.663, "sm": 3000, "em": 3662, "cs": 44, "ce": 56, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "Euribor", "s": 3.675, "e": 4.212, "sm": 3675, "em": 4212, "cs": 57, "ce": 64, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": ",", "s": 4.212, 